WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.040
My first reaction watching
Surviving Paradise

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.240
is how affecting it was.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.560
It's very personal.
It's small.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.840
It's about being
able to empathise

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.760
with the characters
in the documentary,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.280
which is super close

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.800
to what I do every day
at work anyway.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.960
It's all about figuring out

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.720
what we can learn about
the world through empathy,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:18.680
how we can empathise

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.240
with what these animals
are going through,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.520
on a kind of very personal
level, and from that,

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.720
learning what our place
in that drama is.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.640
It's gotta be
the lone mother lioness

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:28.800
when she's hunting,
for her kids,

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:29.800
and fighting off

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.760
the stronger male lions,
all by herself,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.760
just to kinda keep this
little family unit together.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.440
I think that's the kind of
thing that I'm talking about

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.280
that I think people will
relate to quite personally

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.240
on a strange level,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.920
because everyone
knows what it's like

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.920
to be a parent fighting
for your family.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.080
And you know,
people have watched

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.240
their parents
do that for them.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.480
And that's kind of something

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:50.960
that I think can
connect everyone.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:51.920
It's very universal.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.640
I think it was the elephants
being gardeners.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.840
I've never actually kind
of thought of elephants

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.560
as being
particularly helpful.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.120
They tend to kind of
take down trees and crops.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.000
But this idea that they are
gardening the environment,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.520
that they kinda
take down trees

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.040
that create pathways
and waterways

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.040
for the water to flow along
in the wet season

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.200
in the floodplains,

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.320
how they work
with the hippos,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.520
about how all
of that interlinks

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.440
with how they spread seeds
from various fruits.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.440
I think finding how
everything has its purpose

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.240
and its place in maintaining
the environment

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.360
was definitely the coolest
piece that I learnt.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.160
Our central focus
was in creating

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.840
a dramatic narrative

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.440
that an audience
can hold onto

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.280
and relate to
and empathise with.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.440
And so it was all about just
finding the through lines

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:40.840
of what are these animals
going through

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.360
on a personal level.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.520
How does that link
into the wider picture?

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.440
And how can I help
communicate that

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.160
with an audience in a very
visceral and entertaining

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.560
and funny and tragic way

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.680
so that you go on
a whole journey with us?

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.160
It's tricky

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.880
'cause I'm a city kid,
I live in London,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.120
but I think the joy is that
London's a very green city.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.000
And so, I try to get
to the parks whenever I can.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.560
I love watching
the seasons change,

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.280
the leaves
coming off the trees.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.840
I think there's those
unexpected bits of wildlife,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.640
even in urban environments.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.640
London has a population
of parakeets,

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.720
which is the most
unexpected piece of wildlife

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.160
you'd think of
in this part of the world.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.960
I think they might have
escaped a zoo or a film set.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.320
The main thing
I hope audiences

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.640
take away and learn

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.440
is how interconnected
everything is.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.800
How the actions
of one animal

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.520
affects the other animals,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.280
how that affects
the environment,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.600
how the environment then

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.000
feeds back
into those animals,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.160
how what we do affects
the changing environment.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.800
There's the whole section
with the elephants

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.240
and how they have
a generational memory

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.600
that they pass on,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.160
of the routes
that they go to find water,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.200
of the routes that they take
to avoid fires,

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.480
of techniques that
they take to avoid fires,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.000
and how they pass that down.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.240
And how all of that changes,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.160
depending on how
we change their environment.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.880
And so, 80-year-old
knowledge

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.920
that's five generations old
in elephants

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.400
that suddenly
comes under threat because

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.960
those pathways to waters
don't exist anymore

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.160
because of what we've done.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.760
And it's how nothing lives
in isolation anymore.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.680
Everything that we do
outside of the delta

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.160
affects things in the delta.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.560
Everything that happens
in the delta

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.040
affects everything else
in the delta.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.440
So, it's just
that everything's connected

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.280
and there's nowhere
to hide from that, really.

